-- Two policies from 0387 were attached to the wrong parent. Both are dropped,
-- because neither table carries a company at all.
--
-- ── HOW THE MISTAKE WAS MADE, SO IT IS NOT MADE AGAIN ───────────────────────
--
-- 0387's 49 policies were generated by walking the schema for tables carrying
-- `entity_id`, and then for tables carrying a column NAMED like a parent's key
-- — `entry_id`, `invoice_id`, `bill_id`. **A column name is not a foreign key**,
-- and two tables proved it:
--
--   time_entry_dimensions.entry_id  -> time_entries, NOT journal_entries
--   operator_postings.invoice_id    -> nothing; it has no foreign key at all
--
-- The first was caught by CI within minutes: the EXISTS looked for a
-- `journal_entries` row with a TIME entry's id, never found one, and the WITH
-- CHECK refused **every insert** into `time_entry_dimensions`. Five tests, one
-- cause, error 42501 at ExecWithCheckOptions.
--
-- The second was not caught by anything, which is the more useful half of the
-- lesson. `operator_postings` is the OPERATOR's own billing record (Yosher
-- charging its clients, ADR 0041) keyed on `client_tenant_id`; its `invoice_id`
-- is a bare uuid with no declared relationship to `invoices`. Nothing in the
-- suite sets a company scope while reading it, so a policy hiding every row
-- would have sat there until somebody noticed their billing history was empty.
--
-- ── WHY DROP RATHER THAN REPOINT ────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- `time_entries` has no `entity_id` and no path to one: an hour worked is not
-- filed against a set of books. `operator_postings` is not a tenant's books
-- either. Neither table can be company-scoped, so the honest fix is no policy,
-- not a better one.
--
-- Dropping only widens — both tables return to exactly the visibility they had
-- before 0387, which is full tenant scoping and nothing more.
--
-- `tests/entity-scope-policies.test.ts` now asserts what the generator assumed:
-- every entity-scope policy names a parent the child has a REAL foreign key to.
-- It fails on both of these.

DROP POLICY IF EXISTS time_entry_dimensions_entity_scope ON "time_entry_dimensions";
--> statement-breakpoint
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS operator_postings_entity_scope ON "operator_postings";
